  "query_preview": "..An upper low currently centered along the US/Canadian \nborder in far western Montana will continue to move east into North \nDakota by Friday. A well defined trough axis associated with this \nsystem will stay to our north across the Great Basin as the upper \nlow pushes east. The main impact across southeast Arizona will be \nincreased wind speeds through Friday. Expect south-southwest winds \nof 12-20 mph and gusts to 25-35 mph areawide today, with west- \nnorthwest winds of 15-20 mph and gusts to 25-35 mph to the east of \nTucson on Friday. Near critical fire weather conditions are expected \neast of Tucson, mainly in Greenlee, Graham and Cochise counties. \nHigh temperatures today will be a couple degrees above normal, with \nhighs a couple degrees below normal on Friday.\n\nThis weekend through early next week ridging will take place across \nthe Intermountain West, with a strong Pacific upper low sitting off \nthe West Coast. At time same time, surface high pressure will build \ndown the front range of the Rockies into west/central Texas Sunday \ninto Monday, resulting in a strong pressure gradient setting up over \nsoutheast Arizona and western New Mexico. This will bring gusty east \nto southeast winds of 15-20 mph and gusts to 25-35 mph to locations \nprone to easterly winds across southeast Arizona. Winds at this time \nlook the strongest in the Gila River Valley, where southeast winds \nof 20-30 mph and gusts to around 40 mph will be possible. This \neasterly flow will import just enough mid level moisture into \neastern portions of southeast Arizona for a slight chance (10-15 \npercent) of showers and/or thunderstorms Sunday and Monday along the \nNew Mexico state line. High temperatures will warm to 4-7 degrees \nabove normal Saturday through the middle of next week. The latest \nNBM probabilities for 90+ degrees in the Tucson metro are: 38% \nSunday, 25% Monday and 50% on Tuesday.\n\nMid to late next week, the ensemble/deterministic models all bring \nthe Pacific upper low onshore. However, there are differences in the \nstrength, timing and track of the upper low, with these differences \nhighlighted extremely well by Cluster Analysis. For now, cooler \ntemperatures are in the forecast for the latter half of next week. \nDepending upon the evolution of this system, breezy winds and/or the \nchance of precipitation may also be a possibility.  \n\n&",
